Fix heap corruption in the ICE candidate-pair scheduler
(CSteamNetworkingICESession::Think_TestPeerConnectivity).

The function collects candidate "foundations" in a
CUtlVector< std::string >. CUtlVector grows through CUtlMemory, which
reallocs its storage and relocates elements bytewise -- fine for POD, not
for std::string: libstdc++'s small-string optimization stores a pointer to
the string's OWN internal buffer, so a bytewise move leaves that pointer
aimed at the freed old address. Destroying the vector then frees a stale
pointer and glibc aborts the process:

    munmap_chunk(): invalid pointer
    SIGABRT
      CUtlVector<std::string>::RemoveAll() -> ~basic_string -> free
      CSteamNetworkingICESession::Think_TestPeerConnectivity()
      CSteamNetworkingICESession::Think()
      IThinker::Thinker_ProcessThinkers()          [GNS service thread]

Not previously reachable on Linux: this code only runs once ICE has real
candidate pairs, and GetLocalAddresses() -- implemented for Windows only
upstream -- meant no candidates were ever gathered (see
add-posix-local-addresses.patch). Fixing that enumeration exposed this.

Swapped for a std::vector<std::string>, which relocates elements by moving
them properly. Same behaviour otherwise: HasElement -> std::find.

diff --git a/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_stun.cpp b/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_stun.cpp
